window.__courseChapter("v1-01", {"v1-01-00-overview": {"title": "Overview: the big ideas", "summary": "The chapter's key concepts and how its sections fit together.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-01", "sections": ["Purpose", "concept map"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "Welcome to **Introduction** (Vol I). This overview gives you the chapter's big ideas before the section-by-section lessons. Read the claims once, flip the flashcards, then take the warm-up quiz: it is fine to miss questions here, the lessons that follow fill the gaps."}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Introduction, Purpose", "md": "Machine learning systems have a physics. Data moves through memory hierarchies governed by bandwidth, arithmetic executes on silicon governed by power, and predictions must arrive within strict latency windows. Conventional software can often treat such constraints as implementation details, but in ML systems they decide what can run at all."}, {"type": "flow", "title": "The path through this chapter", "nodes": [{"label": "The AI Systems Moment", "detail": "Lesson 1 covers this section."}, {"label": "From Code-Defined Logic to Data-Defined ", "detail": "Lesson 2 covers this section."}, {"label": "The Evolution of AI Bottlenecks", "detail": "Lesson 3 covers this section."}, {"label": "The Bitter Lesson", "detail": "Lesson 4 covers this section."}, {"label": "Defining ML Systems", "detail": "Lesson 5 covers this section."}, {"label": "When ML Systems Fail Silently", "detail": "Lesson 6 covers this section."}, {"label": "Iron Law of ML Systems", "detail": "Lesson 7 covers this section."}]}, {"type": "table", "headers": ["Concept", "The claim to remember"], "rows": [["**Dual mandate**", "Every ML system must show both that its learned behavior is trustworthy and that the machine delivers it within time, memory, energy, and cost budgets, so an accurate but slow model and a fast model trained on unrepresentative data are both failures."], ["**Data defines behavior**", "Training data plus the optimization procedure produce the program, so changing the dataset changes system behavior as surely as changing source code, and debugging, versioning, and review must extend to the data."], ["**D·A·M diagnosis**", "Attributing a symptom to Data, Algorithm, or Machine chooses the evidence and the class of fix, and relieving the limiting constraint moves the bottleneck across the triad, so diagnosis is a loop of diagnose, intervene, and re-measure."], ["**Iron law of ML systems**", "Across Data, Algorithm, and Machine, execution time factors as samples per task times FLOPs per sample times seconds per FLOP (where the first two multiply to total operations O), and unpacks on hardware into bytes moved over bandwidth plus operations over peak rate times utilization plus fixed overhead, with ideal overlap giving the larger of the first two plus overhead, so a faster component helps only through the share of time it changes."], ["**Three efficiency levers**", "Data selection cuts samples per task, algorithmic efficiency lowers FLOPs per sample, and compute efficiency lowers realized seconds per FLOP by raising utilization and reducing stalls, while return on compute (accuracy gained per added cost) tests whether an added investment is worth its price."], ["**Silent degradation**", "ML adds a failure route in which code, runtime, and health checks stay green while predictive quality falls as the operating population shifts or training and serving transforms diverge, and locally accuracy falls by a fitted sensitivity times the divergence (eq-degradation), where divergence prompts investigation and labeled outcomes establish loss."], ["**Bitter lesson and bottleneck migration**", "General methods that turn computation into capability have repeatedly overtaken encoded human knowledge, so each AI era traded one limiting constraint for the next and today's limit is the infrastructure: memory, bandwidth, power, and orchestration."], ["**The system is the model**", "User-visible behavior is produced by the data pipeline, weights, execution stack, serving path, and monitoring loop together, so most of the engineering burden and the costliest failures sit outside the learned parameters, at interfaces no component test exercises."]]}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "key", "title": "Formulas you will meet", "md": "$$\\text{Verification Gap} = \\text{Total Input Space} - \\text{Test Set Coverage} \\approx \\text{Total Input Space}$$\n\n$$\\text{Accuracy}(t) \\approx \\text{Accuracy}_0 - \\lambda \\cdot \\mathcal{D}(P_t \\lVert P_0)$$\n\n$$T = \\left(\\frac{\\text{Samples}}{\\text{Task}}\\right) \\times \\left(\\frac{\\text{FLOPs}}{\\text{Sample}}\\right) \\times \\left(\\frac{\\text{Seconds}}{\\text{FLOP}}\\right)$$\n\n$$T \\approx \\frac{D_{\\text{vol}}}{\\text{BW}} + \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}} + L_{\\text{lat}}$$"}, {"type": "flashcards", "id": "v1-01-cards-concepts", "title": "Key concepts", "cards": [{"front": "Dual mandate", "back": "Every ML system must show both that its learned behavior is trustworthy and that the machine delivers it within time, memory, energy, and cost budgets, so an accurate but slow model and a fast model trained on unrepresentative data are both failures."}, {"front": "Data defines behavior", "back": "Training data plus the optimization procedure produce the program, so changing the dataset changes system behavior as surely as changing source code, and debugging, versioning, and review must extend to the data."}, {"front": "D·A·M diagnosis", "back": "Attributing a symptom to Data, Algorithm, or Machine chooses the evidence and the class of fix, and relieving the limiting constraint moves the bottleneck across the triad, so diagnosis is a loop of diagnose, intervene, and re-measure."}, {"front": "Iron law of ML systems", "back": "Across Data, Algorithm, and Machine, execution time factors as samples per task times FLOPs per sample times seconds per FLOP (where the first two multiply to total operations O), and unpacks on hardware into bytes moved over bandwidth plus operations over peak rate times utilization plus fixed overhead, with ideal overlap giving the larger of the first two plus overhead, so a faster component helps only through the share of time it changes."}, {"front": "Three efficiency levers", "back": "Data selection cuts samples per task, algorithmic efficiency lowers FLOPs per sample, and compute efficiency lowers realized seconds per FLOP by raising utilization and reducing stalls, while return on compute (accuracy gained per added cost) tests whether an added investment is worth its price."}, {"front": "Silent degradation", "back": "ML adds a failure route in which code, runtime, and health checks stay green while predictive quality falls as the operating population shifts or training and serving transforms diverge, and locally accuracy falls by a fitted sensitivity times the divergence (eq-degradation), where divergence prompts investigation and labeled outcomes establish loss."}, {"front": "Bitter lesson and bottleneck migration", "back": "General methods that turn computation into capability have repeatedly overtaken encoded human knowledge, so each AI era traded one limiting constraint for the next and today's limit is the infrastructure: memory, bandwidth, power, and orchestration."}, {"front": "The system is the model", "back": "User-visible behavior is produced by the data pipeline, weights, execution stack, serving path, and monitoring loop together, so most of the engineering burden and the costliest failures sit outside the learned parameters, at interfaces no component test exercises."}]}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-01-quiz-warmup", "title": "Warm-up: match the big ideas", "questions": [{"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Dual mandate**?", "options": ["User-visible behavior is produced by the data pipeline, weights, execution stack, serving path, and monitoring loop together, so most of the engineering burden and the costliest failures sit outside the learned parameters, at interfaces no component test exercises.", "Training data plus the optimization procedure produce the program, so changing the dataset changes system behavior as surely as changing source code, and debugging, versioning, and review must extend to the data.", "Every ML system must show both that its learned behavior is trustworthy and that the machine delivers it within time, memory, energy, and cost budgets, so an accurate but slow model and a fast model trained on unrepresentative data are both failures.", "General methods that turn computation into capability have repeatedly overtaken encoded human knowledge, so each AI era traded one limiting constraint for the next and today's limit is the infrastructure: memory, bandwidth, power, and orchestration."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "**Dual mandate:** Every ML system must show both that its learned behavior is trustworthy and that the machine delivers it within time, memory, energy, and cost budgets, so an accurate but slow model and a fast model trained on unrepresentative data are both failures.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Dual mandate itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Data defines behavior**?", "options": ["Training data plus the optimization procedure produce the program, so changing the dataset changes system behavior as surely as changing source code, and debugging, versioning, and review must extend to the data.", "General methods that turn computation into capability have repeatedly overtaken encoded human knowledge, so each AI era traded one limiting constraint for the next and today's limit is the infrastructure: memory, bandwidth, power, and orchestration.", "Attributing a symptom to Data, Algorithm, or Machine chooses the evidence and the class of fix, and relieving the limiting constraint moves the bottleneck across the triad, so diagnosis is a loop of diagnose, intervene, and re-measure.", "ML adds a failure route in which code, runtime, and health checks stay green while predictive quality falls as the operating population shifts or training and serving transforms diverge, and locally accuracy falls by a fitted sensitivity times the divergence (eq-degradation), where divergence prompts investigation and labeled outcomes establish loss."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "**Data defines behavior:** Training data plus the optimization procedure produce the program, so changing the dataset changes system behavior as surely as changing source code, and debugging, versioning, and review must extend to the data.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Data defines behavior itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **D·A·M diagnosis**?", "options": ["Across Data, Algorithm, and Machine, execution time factors as samples per task times FLOPs per sample times seconds per FLOP (where the first two multiply to total operations O), and unpacks on hardware into bytes moved over bandwidth plus operations over peak rate times utilization plus fixed overhead, with ideal overlap giving the larger of the first two plus overhead, so a faster component helps only through the share of time it changes.", "General methods that turn computation into capability have repeatedly overtaken encoded human knowledge, so each AI era traded one limiting constraint for the next and today's limit is the infrastructure: memory, bandwidth, power, and orchestration.", "Every ML system must show both that its learned behavior is trustworthy and that the machine delivers it within time, memory, energy, and cost budgets, so an accurate but slow model and a fast model trained on unrepresentative data are both failures.", "Attributing a symptom to Data, Algorithm, or Machine chooses the evidence and the class of fix, and relieving the limiting constraint moves the bottleneck across the triad, so diagnosis is a loop of diagnose, intervene, and re-measure."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**D·A·M diagnosis:** Attributing a symptom to Data, Algorithm, or Machine chooses the evidence and the class of fix, and relieving the limiting constraint moves the bottleneck across the triad, so diagnosis is a loop of diagnose, intervene, and re-measure.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is D·A·M diagnosis itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Iron law of ML systems**?", "options": ["ML adds a failure route in which code, runtime, and health checks stay green while predictive quality falls as the operating population shifts or training and serving transforms diverge, and locally accuracy falls by a fitted sensitivity times the divergence (eq-degradation), where divergence prompts investigation and labeled outcomes establish loss.", "Training data plus the optimization procedure produce the program, so changing the dataset changes system behavior as surely as changing source code, and debugging, versioning, and review must extend to the data.", "Across Data, Algorithm, and Machine, execution time factors as samples per task times FLOPs per sample times seconds per FLOP (where the first two multiply to total operations O), and unpacks on hardware into bytes moved over bandwidth plus operations over peak rate times utilization plus fixed overhead, with ideal overlap giving the larger of the first two plus overhead, so a faster component helps only through the share of time it changes.", "Data selection cuts samples per task, algorithmic efficiency lowers FLOPs per sample, and compute efficiency lowers realized seconds per FLOP by raising utilization and reducing stalls, while return on compute (accuracy gained per added cost) tests whether an added investment is worth its price."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "**Iron law of ML systems:** Across Data, Algorithm, and Machine, execution time factors as samples per task times FLOPs per sample times seconds per FLOP (where the first two multiply to total operations O), and unpacks on hardware into bytes moved over bandwidth plus operations over peak rate times utilization plus fixed overhead, with ideal overlap giving the larger of the first two plus overhead, so a faster component helps only through the share of time it changes.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Iron law of ML systems itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Three efficiency levers**?", "options": ["User-visible behavior is produced by the data pipeline, weights, execution stack, serving path, and monitoring loop together, so most of the engineering burden and the costliest failures sit outside the learned parameters, at interfaces no component test exercises.", "Training data plus the optimization procedure produce the program, so changing the dataset changes system behavior as surely as changing source code, and debugging, versioning, and review must extend to the data.", "ML adds a failure route in which code, runtime, and health checks stay green while predictive quality falls as the operating population shifts or training and serving transforms diverge, and locally accuracy falls by a fitted sensitivity times the divergence (eq-degradation), where divergence prompts investigation and labeled outcomes establish loss.", "Data selection cuts samples per task, algorithmic efficiency lowers FLOPs per sample, and compute efficiency lowers realized seconds per FLOP by raising utilization and reducing stalls, while return on compute (accuracy gained per added cost) tests whether an added investment is worth its price."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Three efficiency levers:** Data selection cuts samples per task, algorithmic efficiency lowers FLOPs per sample, and compute efficiency lowers realized seconds per FLOP by raising utilization and reducing stalls, while return on compute (accuracy gained per added cost) tests whether an added investment is worth its price.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Three efficiency levers itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["Dual mandate: Every ML system must show both that its learned behavior is trustworthy and that the machine delivers it within time, memory, energy, and cost budgets, so an accurate but slow model and a fast model trained on unrepresentative data are both failures.", "Data defines behavior: Training data plus the optimization procedure produce the program, so changing the dataset changes system behavior as surely as changing source code, and debugging, versioning, and review must extend to the data.", "D·A·M diagnosis: Attributing a symptom to Data, Algorithm, or Machine chooses the evidence and the class of fix, and relieving the limiting constraint moves the bottleneck across the triad, so diagnosis is a loop of diagnose, intervene, and re-measure.", "Iron law of ML systems: Across Data, Algorithm, and Machine, execution time factors as samples per task times FLOPs per sample times seconds per FLOP (where the first two multiply to total operations O), and unpacks on hardware into bytes moved over bandwidth plus operations over peak rate times utilization plus fixed overhead, with ideal overlap giving the larger of the first two plus overhead, so a faster component helps only through the share of time it changes."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Dual mandate", "def": "Every ML system must show both that its learned behavior is trustworthy and that the machine delivers it within time, memory, energy, and cost budgets, so an accurate but slow model and a fast model trained on unrepresentative data are both failures."}, {"term": "Data defines behavior", "def": "Training data plus the optimization procedure produce the program, so changing the dataset changes system behavior as surely as changing source code, and debugging, versioning, and review must extend to the data."}, {"term": "D·A·M diagnosis", "def": "Attributing a symptom to Data, Algorithm, or Machine chooses the evidence and the class of fix, and relieving the limiting constraint moves the bottleneck across the triad, so diagnosis is a loop of diagnose, intervene, and re-measure."}, {"term": "Iron law of ML systems", "def": "Across Data, Algorithm, and Machine, execution time factors as samples per task times FLOPs per sample times seconds per FLOP (where the first two multiply to total operations O), and unpacks on hardware into bytes moved over bandwidth plus operations over peak rate times utilization plus fixed overhead, with ideal overlap giving the larger of the first two plus overhead, so a faster component helps only through the share of time it changes."}, {"term": "Three efficiency levers", "def": "Data selection cuts samples per task, algorithmic efficiency lowers FLOPs per sample, and compute efficiency lowers realized seconds per FLOP by raising utilization and reducing stalls, while return on compute (accuracy gained per added cost) tests whether an added investment is worth its price."}, {"term": "Silent degradation", "def": "ML adds a failure route in which code, runtime, and health checks stay green while predictive quality falls as the operating population shifts or training and serving transforms diverge, and locally accuracy falls by a fitted sensitivity times the divergence (eq-degradation), where divergence prompts investigation and labeled outcomes establish loss."}, {"term": "Bitter lesson and bottleneck migration", "def": "General methods that turn computation into capability have repeatedly overtaken encoded human knowledge, so each AI era traded one limiting constraint for the next and today's limit is the infrastructure: memory, bandwidth, power, and orchestration."}, {"term": "The system is the model", "def": "User-visible behavior is produced by the data pipeline, weights, execution stack, serving path, and monitoring loop together, so most of the engineering burden and the costliest failures sit outside the learned parameters, at interfaces no component test exercises."}], "id": "v1-01-00-overview", "chapter": "v1-01", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 10}, "v1-01-01-the-ai-systems-moment": {"title": "The AI Systems Moment", "summary": "Distinguish between physical execution failures and statistical trustworthiness failures within the dual-mandate framework.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-01", "sections": ["The AI Systems Moment"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **The AI Systems Moment** in *Introduction* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Distinguish between physical execution failures and statistical trustworthiness failures within the dual-mandate framework\n- Explain why traditional software health metrics such as uptime and exception logging are insufficient to verify ML system correctness\n- Analyze why data-defined behavior transforms AI deployment into a full-stack orchestration challenge\n- Identify the foundational term defining the dual constraints of ML systems engineering"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Introduction, § The AI Systems Moment", "md": "A spoken question to a smartphone voice assistant sets off a chain of learned components. One model converts the audio to text, another infers what the user wants, a retrieval service gathers relevant information, and a generative model composes the reply, all within a few seconds and across a phone, a network, and a data center. Each stage runs under its own memory, latency, and energy budget, and a delay or error at any stage reaches the user."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "Pay attention to: dual mandate of statistical uncertainty and physical constraints; three converging forces driving modern AI scale; silent vs loud failure modes in production ML systems."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Why does the shift from code-defined logic to data-defined behavior turn large-scale AI deployment into an end-to-end orchestration problem rather than an isolated modeling exercise?", "md": "When data defines system behavior, correctness depends on continuous data pipelines, feature consistency, and representative distributions rather than static code logic alone. Deploying these models at scale requires coordinating distributed accelerator clusters, memory hierarchies, and real-world latency budgets while managing the risk of silent statistical decay under shifting operational data."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-01-01-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A production autonomous vehicle perception model reliably detects obstacles under clear daytime conditions, but fails to identify a pedestrian at dusk even though all code executes without runtime exceptions and strictly within its 50 ms latency window. Which core challenge of ML systems engineering does this failure demonstrate?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The dual mandate requires that an ML system satisfy both physical constraints (latency, compute, memory, power) and statistical trustworthiness (accurate, reliable predictions across operational domains). In this scenario, the perception stack met its physical execution budget (running in under 50 ms without crashing) but failed its statistical obligation because its learned representations did not generalize to dusk conditions. Thermal throttling or serial interconnect latency would manifest as latency spikes rather than silent perceptual misses. Compiling stochastic gradient descent relates to training pipelines rather than inference execution.", "type": "single", "options": ["The latency added by serial interconnect communication across distributed accelerators", "A hardware execution failure caused by GPU thermal throttling and clock frequency scaling", "The dual mandate requirement that learned behavior must remain statistically trustworthy while executing within physical execution constraints", "The failure of traditional continuous integration pipelines to compile stochastic gradient descent operations"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: distinguish between physical execution failures and statistical trustworthiness failures within the dual-mandate framework."]}, {"q": "In a production ML system, if all code executes without runtime exceptions, CPU and GPU utilization are normal, and latency requirements are met, the system is guaranteed to be operating correctly.", "explanation": "False. Healthy execution telemetry shows that the software and hardware path is functioning, but it does not guarantee good predictions. In an ML system, the code can run correctly and on time while the live data has drifted away from the training distribution, causing silent degradation in prediction quality.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: explain why traditional software health metrics such as uptime and exception logging are insufficient to verify ML system correctness."]}, {"q": "The fundamental engineering obligation requiring that an ML system's learned behavior be statistically trustworthy while the machine delivers that behavior within finite time, memory, energy, and cost budgets is known as the ____.", "explanation": "dual mandate. It names the requirement to engineer trustworthy learned behavior together with feasible physical execution within the available budgets.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["dual mandate"], "hints": ["Think about: identify the foundational term defining the dual constraints of ML systems engineering."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now distinguish between physical execution failures and statistical trustworthiness failures within the dual-mandate framework.", "You can now explain why traditional software health metrics such as uptime and exception logging are insufficient to verify ML system correctness.", "You can now analyze why data-defined behavior transforms AI deployment into a full-stack orchestration challenge.", "You can now identify the foundational term defining the dual constraints of ML systems engineering."]}], "id": "v1-01-01-the-ai-systems-moment", "chapter": "v1-01", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-01-02-from-code-defined-logic-to-data": {"title": "From Code-Defined Logic to Data-Defined Behavior", "summary": "Compare the structural components of Software 1.0 with their Software 2.0 counterparts.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-01", "sections": ["From Code-Defined Logic to Data-Defined Behavior"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **From Code-Defined Logic to Data-Defined Behavior** in *Introduction* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Compare the structural components of Software 1.0 with their Software 2.0 counterparts\n- Calculate and explain the mathematical origin of the verification gap in high-dimensional ML systems\n- Analyze the failure mechanism of Google Flu Trends to evaluate the risks of uncalibrated behavioral proxies\n- Identify the term for data-centric AI versus model-centric AI"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Introduction, § From Code-Defined Logic to Data-Defined Behavior", "md": "In traditional software engineering, a programmer encodes domain logic directly into source code: a tax calculator applies statutory brackets line by line, and an early email filter blocks messages that match a list of forbidden keywords. That rule-based approach works when the specification is closed, but it breaks as soon as attackers rephrase their messages or disguise trigger words faster than engineers can patch the rules."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "Pay attention to: Software 1.0 vs Software 2.0 mapping; verification gap and high-dimensional input space; behavioral proxy drift and Google Flu Trends war story."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: How did Google Flu Trends fail despite having access to hundreds of billions of real-time search queries, and what systems engineering lesson does this failure provide regarding behavioral proxies?", "md": "Google Flu Trends failed because search query volume was a behavioral proxy reflecting news coverage and search autocomplete features (public attention) as well as actual influenza infection (clinical ground truth), causing overestimates for 100 out of 108 weeks. The systems lesson is that massive data volume cannot substitute for a feedback loop to validated ground-truth measurements (such as CDC clinical sentinel data) to continuously detect proxy drift."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-01-02-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "In Andrej Karpathy's Software 1.0 vs. Software 2.0 framing, how do the roles of source code, the compiler, and debugging map to machine learning workflows?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. In Software 2.0, learned weights shaped by training data act as source code, and the training loop (stochastic gradient descent) plays the role of the compiler that produces those weights from the curated examples. Debugging still traces execution, but it also expands to the data and the learned behavior. Treating Python scripts or raw datasets as source code misplaces where the program logic lives, and treating hardware or serving runtimes as compilers confuses the execution platform with the training process that produces the weights.", "type": "single", "options": ["Learned weights shaped by training data act as source code, the training loop (stochastic gradient descent) acts as the compiler, and debugging expands from execution traces to the data and learned behavior.", "Python scripts act as source code, the deep learning framework acts as the compiler, and debugging focuses on stepping through tensor operations in an interactive debugger.", "Training datasets act as source code, GPU hardware acts as the compiler, and debugging focuses on profiling memory bandwidth utilization.", "Pretrained model weights act as source code, inference serving runtimes act as the compiler, and debugging focuses on network packet inspection."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: compare the structural components of Software 1.0 with their Software 2.0 counterparts."]}, {"q": "A computer vision test suite evaluates a \\$224×224 RGB image classifier on 50,000 validation images. Why does passing 100% of these test cases still leave a substantial 'verification gap' in production?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The verification gap, the disparity between what a test set covers and the inputs a deployed system meets, arises because the input space of \\$224{\\times}224$ 8-bit RGB images contains \\$256^{150{,}528}$ possible configurations (a number with over 300,000 digits in base 10), whereas a 50,000-image test set evaluates a vanishingly small fraction. Predeployment testing provides statistical evidence over sampled inputs, not exhaustive mathematical proof. Explanations invoking integer precision describe quantization effects rather than the fundamental input-space disparity. Explanations suggesting batch-size limits or backward pass requirements in production confuse inference serving with training mechanics.", "type": "single", "options": ["Validation sets evaluate floating-point weights, whereas production inference engines always run in integer precision.", "The total input space of possible pixel configurations (\\$256^150,528, spanning over 300,000 decimal digits) vastly exceeds the sample coverage of any finite test set, making exhaustive testing mathematically impossible.", "Convolutional neural networks cannot generalize beyond the exact batch size used during validation testing.", "Test suites only evaluate forward inference passes, whereas production systems must continuously execute backward gradient updates."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: calculate and explain the mathematical origin of the verification gap in high-dimensional ML systems."]}, {"q": "The development paradigm where engineering teams hold model architecture code relatively fixed and systematically improve dataset quality, labels, and coverage to program model behavior is known as ____ AI.", "explanation": "data-centric. It emphasizes improving the data while holding the code comparatively fixed to shape model behavior.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["data centric", "data-centric"], "hints": ["Think about: identify the term for data-centric AI versus model-centric AI."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now compare the structural components of Software 1.0 with their Software 2.0 counterparts.", "You can now calculate and explain the mathematical origin of the verification gap in high-dimensional ML systems.", "You can now analyze the failure mechanism of Google Flu Trends to evaluate the risks of uncalibrated behavioral proxies.", "You can now identify the term for data-centric AI versus model-centric AI."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Training data", "def": "Examples used to fit model parameters; their coverage and labels help determine learned behavior."}], "id": "v1-01-02-from-code-defined-logic-to-data", "chapter": "v1-01", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-01-03-the-evolution-of-ai-bottlenecks": {"title": "The Evolution of AI Bottlenecks", "summary": "Compare the four historical AI eras across their primary limiting systems bottlenecks.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-01", "sections": ["The Evolution of AI Bottlenecks", "Rule-based approaches: Logic and knowledge bottlenecks", "The statistical learning era and the feature engineering bottleneck", "The deep learning era and the infrastructure bottleneck"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **The Evolution of AI Bottlenecks** in *Introduction* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Compare the four historical AI eras across their primary limiting systems bottlenecks\n- Apply Moravec's paradox to explain why perceptual AI workloads drive modern hardware accelerator design\n- Classify the chronological progression of AI engineering eras and their respective systems bottlenecks\n- Evaluate AlexNet as an achievement of systems co-design linking architecture, dataset scale, and GPU hardware\n- Explain how cascaded classifiers and hand-engineered features enabled real-time inference during the statistical learning era"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Introduction, § The Evolution of AI Bottlenecks", "md": "When Alan Turing proposed evaluating machine intelligence through observable responses, computing machinery had little memory or processing capacity, and every early attempt to build intelligent behavior ran into a structural ceiling. Early researchers pursued two contrasting paths: Frank Rosenblatt's Perceptron (1958) attempted to learn weights directly from sensory inputs, whereas Joseph Weizenbaum's ELIZA executed hand-coded pattern substitutions. Both soon met hard limits."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Rule-based approaches: Logic and knowledge bottlenecks\n- The statistical learning era and the feature engineering bottleneck\n- The deep learning era and the infrastructure bottleneck\n\nPay attention to: progression of AI bottlenecks from logic to infrastructure; Moravec's paradox and its hardware accelerator implications; AlexNet as a landmark in systems co-design."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Why was AlexNet's 2012 ImageNet victory considered a breakthrough in systems co-design rather than purely an algorithmic advance?", "md": "AlexNet co-designed the convolutional neural network architecture with the physical hardware constraints of two GTX 580 GPUs, splitting convolutional and dense layers across parallel GPU streams. While convolutional networks had existed for more than a decade, AlexNet aligned dense matrix arithmetic directly with parallel GPU architectures and massive labeled data (ImageNet), achieving a 15.3% top-5 error rate (a 42% relative improvement over the 26.2% runner-up)."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-01-03-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Which historical transition correctly pairs an AI era with the primary systems bottleneck that limited its scalability and forced the transition to the subsequent paradigm?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Expert systems hit the knowledge acquisition bottleneck because extracting and maintaining consistent rules was bound by the serial bandwidth of human experts; statistical learning overcame this by estimating probabilities from data, but hit the feature engineering bottleneck because humans still had to manually design feature extractors (e.g., SIFT, HOG); deep learning overcame this by learning representations end-to-end from raw data. Compute throughput and GPU memory bandwidth constrained deep learning, not early symbolic or expert systems. Formal logic ambiguity was the logic bottleneck of symbolic AI, not statistical learning.", "type": "single", "options": ["Symbolic AI was limited by compute throughput, forcing the transition to expert systems; Deep Learning was limited by human rule maintenance, forcing the transition to statistical learning.", "Expert Systems were limited by GPU memory bandwidth, forcing the transition to statistical learning; Statistical Learning was limited by formal logic ambiguity, forcing the transition to deep learning.", "Statistical Learning was limited by a complete lack of training labels, forcing the transition to symbolic logic; Symbolic AI was limited by hardware integer arithmetic, forcing the transition to neural networks.", "Expert Systems were limited by the knowledge acquisition bottleneck (serial human expert elicitation bandwidth), forcing the transition to statistical learning; Statistical Learning was limited by the feature engineering bottleneck (manual extraction of hand-crafted representations), forcing the transition to deep learning."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: compare the four historical AI eras across their primary limiting systems bottlenecks."]}, {"q": "Moravec's paradox observes that tasks humans find easy (such as visual perception, walking, and grasping) require vast computational resources, while tasks humans find hard (such as playing chess or solving algebra) require comparatively little compute. What is the direct implication of this paradox for ML systems hardware?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Moravec's paradox explains why perception, vision, and motor control—which humans execute effortlessly—require processing high-dimensional data at high frame rates, driving the requirement for massive arithmetic parallelism, high memory bandwidth, and domain-specific accelerators (GPUs, TPUs). The claim that symbolic reasoning requires accelerator clusters reverses the computational requirements. The assertion that high-level reasoning saturates bandwidth while perception is only compute-bound ignores the massive data movement required for continuous video streams. Microcontroller deployment for perception requires aggressive compression due to strict hardware limits.", "type": "single", "options": ["Symbolic reasoning algorithms require multi-GPU accelerator clusters, whereas computer vision pipelines run efficiently on single-threaded CPUs.", "High-level reasoning tasks saturate off-chip memory bandwidth, while low-level perceptual tasks are strictly compute-bound.", "Perceptual and physical-world AI tasks demand massive parallelism, high memory bandwidth, and specialized hardware accelerators to process dense, high-dimensional sensor streams in real time.", "Robotic perception models can be deployed on microcontrollers without model compression or accuracy degradation."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: apply Moravec's paradox to explain why perceptual AI workloads drive modern hardware accelerator design."]}, {"q": "Place the four historical AI engineering eras in chronological order based on when their primary paradigm dominated, and identify the key bottleneck that constrained each era:", "explanation": "The correct order is (3) -> (2) -> (4) -> (1).\n- (3) Symbolic AI Era (1950s–1970s): Constrained by the logic bottleneck (brittle hand-coded rules unable to handle real-world ambiguity).\n- (2) Expert Systems Era (1970s–1980s): Constrained by the knowledge acquisition bottleneck (serial human expert elicitation bandwidth).\n- (4) Statistical Learning Era (1990s–2000s): Constrained by the feature engineering bottleneck (manual extraction of hand-crafted features prior to statistical classification).\n- (1) Deep Learning Era (2010s–present): Constrained by the compute and infrastructure bottleneck (hardware scaling, memory bandwidth, and distributed coordination).", "type": "order", "items": ["Symbolic AI Era", "Expert Systems Era", "Statistical Learning Era", "Deep Learning Era"], "hints": ["Think about: classify the chronological progression of AI engineering eras and their respective systems bottlenecks."]}, {"q": "The Viola-Jones face detection algorithm achieved real-time execution on early-2000s CPUs by using a cascade of simple rectangular features that evaluated an average of about ten features per candidate region, although the full cascade contained over 6,000 features.", "explanation": "True. Viola-Jones exemplified the statistical learning era: expert feature engineering (integral image rectangular features) and a cascaded classifier allowed early rejection of non-face regions, achieving real-time performance within narrow domains while remaining constrained by manual feature engineering when applied to new tasks.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": true, "hints": ["Think about: explain how cascaded classifiers and hand-engineered features enabled real-time inference during the statistical learning era."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now compare the four historical AI eras across their primary limiting systems bottlenecks.", "You can now apply Moravec's paradox to explain why perceptual AI workloads drive modern hardware accelerator design.", "You can now classify the chronological progression of AI engineering eras and their respective systems bottlenecks.", "You can now evaluate AlexNet as an achievement of systems co-design linking architecture, dataset scale, and GPU hardware."]}], "id": "v1-01-03-the-evolution-of-ai-bottlenecks", "chapter": "v1-01", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-01-04-the-bitter-lesson": {"title": "The Bitter Lesson", "summary": "Explain why the bitter lesson prioritizes scalable computation and learning over hand-crafted human domain expertise.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-01", "sections": ["The Bitter Lesson"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **The Bitter Lesson** in *Introduction* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Explain why the bitter lesson prioritizes scalable computation and learning over hand-crafted human domain expertise\n- Compare how Deep Blue and AlphaGo Zero balanced hardware acceleration, search scale, and learned representations\n- Justify why systems engineering is the prerequisite for realizing the benefits of the bitter lesson\n- Evaluate the long-term trade-off between domain-specific inductive biases and general scalable architectures under expanding compute budgets"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Introduction, § The Bitter Lesson", "md": "Expert systems invested engineering effort in encoding domain knowledge; deep learning systems invest much of that effort in learning from data at scale. The **bitter lesson** captures the historical advantage of general methods, especially search and learning, that can make effective use of increasing computation. Richard Sutton crystallized this insight in his 2019 essay \"The Bitter Lesson\". Sutton wrote, \"The biggest lesson that can be read from 70 years of AI research is that general methods that leverage computation are ultimately the most effective, and by a large margin.\""}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "Pay attention to: Sutton's Bitter Lesson thesis (general methods + compute vs encoded human expertise); historical validation (Deep Blue, AlphaGo, LLMs); systems engineering as the prerequisite to absorb computational scale."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: If the bitter lesson states that computation-leveraging methods dominate over time, why does realizing this advantage depend primarily on systems engineering rather than pure algorithmic theory?", "md": "Harnessing computation at scale requires solving physical systems bottlenecks: memory bandwidth, cluster interconnects, distributed fault tolerance, thermal dissipation, and gigawatt-hour energy budgets ($E_{\\text{move}} \\gg E_{\\text{compute}}$). An algorithm designed to scale with compute is ineffective if memory systems cannot supply weights fast enough or if infrastructure cannot coordinate thousands of accelerators without stalling."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-01-04-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Why did Richard Sutton describe the fundamental finding of 70 years of AI research as a 'bitter' lesson for researchers and engineers?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The lesson is 'bitter' because researchers persistently try to hand-craft human domain heuristics (such as chess evaluation tables, linguistic grammars, or hand-tuned visual filters), only to discover that general methods (search and learning) powered by massive computation consistently surpass hand-engineered representations as scale increases. Thermodynamic scaling limits describe hardware physical bounds rather than Sutton's philosophical thesis. Lack of formal verification concerns how learned systems are checked, not Sutton's thesis. The comparison between open-source and proprietary models is a market dynamic unrelated to Sutton's essay.", "type": "single", "options": ["Human intuition naturally seeks to build intelligence by encoding domain expertise and linguistic rules into models, yet historical progress repeatedly demonstrates that general-purpose search and learning leveraging raw computation outperform hand-crafted human knowledge.", "Hardware accelerators have reached physical thermodynamic scaling limits, preventing further increases in neural network parameter counts.", "Stochastic gradient descent algorithms produce models whose internal mathematical representations cannot be formally proven correct.", "Open-source models consistently match the performance of proprietary industrial foundation models trained at hundred-million-dollar compute budgets."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: explain why the bitter lesson prioritizes scalable computation and learning over hand-crafted human domain expertise."]}, {"q": "In comparing IBM's Deep Blue (1997) and DeepMind's AlphaGo Zero (2017), how do their designs reflect the progression toward Sutton's bitter lesson?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Deep Blue paired custom hardware search (480 custom chess chips searching roughly 100 to 200 million positions per second) with chess-specific evaluation knowledge. AlphaGo Zero advanced this trajectory by learning through self-play without human game records or hand-engineered features, using neural networks to guide tree search so that computation, rather than encoded human knowledge, improved play. The claim that Deep Blue used deep reinforcement learning reverses the historical paradigms. The assertion that neither system used specialized compute contradicts the custom silicon of Deep Blue and the large-scale self-play computation of AlphaGo Zero. AlphaGo Zero used neural networks to guide tree search rather than eliminating search.", "type": "single", "options": ["Deep Blue relied entirely on deep reinforcement learning, whereas AlphaGo Zero returned to hand-coded expert evaluation tables.", "Deep Blue combined custom silicon search (100 to 200 million positions/second) with hand-coded chess heuristics, whereas AlphaGo Zero learned through self-play without human game records or hand-engineered features, using neural networks to guide tree search.", "Both systems avoided the use of custom silicon or GPUs, relying strictly on algorithmic elegance over compute scale.", "AlphaGo Zero eliminated all tree search mechanisms in favor of pure single-step feedforward classification."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: compare how Deep Blue and AlphaGo Zero balanced hardware acceleration, search scale, and learned representations."]}, {"q": "According to the bitter lesson, building domain-specific linguistic or perceptual rules into deep neural network architectures provides a permanent, compounding advantage over general architectures as compute budgets expand.", "explanation": "False. Sutton's bitter lesson demonstrates that domain-specific human heuristics are a depreciating asset; as computational scale increases by orders of magnitude, general architectures (such as transformers) that leverage raw compute and learning consistently surpass specialized, rule-infused designs.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the long-term trade-off between domain-specific inductive biases and general scalable architectures under expanding compute budgets."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now explain why the bitter lesson prioritizes scalable computation and learning over hand-crafted human domain expertise.", "You can now compare how Deep Blue and AlphaGo Zero balanced hardware acceleration, search scale, and learned representations.", "You can now justify why systems engineering is the prerequisite for realizing the benefits of the bitter lesson.", "You can now evaluate the long-term trade-off between domain-specific inductive biases and general scalable architectures under expanding compute budgets."]}], "id": "v1-01-04-the-bitter-lesson", "chapter": "v1-01", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-01-05-defining-ml-systems": {"title": "Defining ML Systems", "summary": "Analyze the limiting constraint in an ML system using the D·A·M taxonomy and identify the corresponding optimization intersection.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-01", "sections": ["Defining ML Systems"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Defining ML Systems** in *Introduction* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze the limiting constraint in an ML system using the D·A·M taxonomy and identify the corresponding optimization intersection\n- Explain the principle of the limiting constraint and the consequences of optimizing components that are not limiting\n- Identify the core engineering focus of the Algorithm-Machine intersection in the D·A·M taxonomy"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Introduction, § Defining ML Systems", "md": "Absorbing computational growth takes a working system, not only a trained model. Return to the email spam filter introduced in a later section: in a notebook, it is a classifier that maps message text to a spam probability, but in production it must process global email traffic measured in hundreds of billions of messages per day. At that scale, the classifier is only one component inside an end-to-end operational pipeline that must continuously ingest new examples, retrain as attack tactics evolve, and classify incoming mail within strict delivery latency budgets."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "Pay attention to: definition of ML systems and limiting constraints; D·A·M taxonomy and intersection landscape (D∩A, D∩M, A∩M, D∩A∩M)."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain what the concept of a 'limiting constraint' means in the D·A·M framework, and describe the risk of optimizing an axis that is not limiting.", "md": "A limiting constraint is the specific physical, algorithmic, or data bottleneck whose relaxation directly improves end-to-end system performance (e.g., latency, throughput, or cost). Optimizing an axis that is not limiting (such as upgrading to faster GPUs when the system is bounded by disk I/O, or collecting more data when model capacity is saturated) expends engineering resources while leaving overall system throughput or prediction quality virtually unchanged."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-01-05-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "An ML engineering team trains a 70-billion-parameter language model. When profiling the distributed cluster, they notice that accelerator compute engines remain idle for 45% of execution time waiting for batch tensors to be loaded from remote object storage over the network. Along which D·A·M axis does the primary limiting constraint lie, and which intersection represents the appropriate optimization space?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The bottleneck is data movement and storage throughput starving the compute engines, placing the constraint along the Data axis. The corresponding design space is the $\\text{Data} \\cap \\text{Machine}$ intersection ('How to Move Information'), which includes I/O bandwidth optimization, asynchronous prefetching, efficient storage formats, and memory hierarchy management. Optimizing mixed precision quantization ($\\text{A} \\cap \\text{M}$) accelerates compute execution but does not resolve storage starvation. Curriculum learning ($\\text{D} \\cap \\text{A}$) selects which samples to present but does not fix I/O pipeline bandwidth.", "type": "single", "options": ["Machine axis; $\\text{Algorithm} \\cap \\text{Machine}$ (mixed precision quantization and kernel fusion)", "Algorithm axis; $\\text{Data} \\cap \\text{Algorithm}$ (curriculum learning and active data selection)", "Data axis; $\\text{Data} \\cap \\text{Machine}$ (I/O pipelining, prefetching, and storage memory hierarchy)", "Workload axis; $\\text{Data} \\cap \\text{Algorithm} \\cap \\text{Machine}$ (reinforcement learning from human feedback)"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the limiting constraint in an ML system using the D·A·M taxonomy and identify the corresponding optimization intersection."]}, {"q": "In the D·A·M intersection landscape, the intersection between Algorithm and Machine ($\\text{A} \\cap \\text{M}$) addresses the core question of 'How to ____', focusing on how the model's mathematical work is mapped onto hardware execution and data movement efficiently.", "explanation": "Execute Efficiently. In the D·A·M taxonomy, the Algorithm-Machine intersection (A ∩ M) asks how the model's computation is carried out on physical hardware so arithmetic and movement are organized efficiently.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["Execute Efficiently"], "hints": ["Think about: identify the core engineering focus of the Algorithm-Machine intersection in the D·A·M taxonomy."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze the limiting constraint in an ML system using the D·A·M taxonomy and identify the corresponding optimization intersection.", "You can now explain the principle of the limiting constraint and the consequences of optimizing components that are not limiting.", "You can now identify the core engineering focus of the Algorithm-Machine intersection in the D·A·M taxonomy."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Learned behavior", "def": "The predictions a trained model produces on inputs, whose usefulness and physical deliverability must both be established."}], "id": "v1-01-05-defining-ml-systems", "chapter": "v1-01", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-01-06-when-ml-systems-fail-silently": {"title": "When ML Systems Fail Silently", "summary": "Analyze the mathematical terms of the degradation equation and map them to their corresponding engineering interventions.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-01", "sections": ["When ML Systems Fail Silently"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **When ML Systems Fail Silently** in *Introduction* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze the mathematical terms of the degradation equation and map them to their corresponding engineering interventions\n- Identify and diagnose training-serving skew as a structural cause of silent degradation in ML systems\n- Explain why drift monitoring must be paired with outcome evaluation to justify model retraining decisions\n- Distinguish between baseline accuracy improvements and distribution shift sensitivity in ML model degradation"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Introduction, § When ML Systems Fail Silently", "md": "Even when every component executes without error, a shift on the data axis can still degrade predictions silently. That vulnerability governs the first half of the dual mandate: keeping learned behavior trustworthy after deployment. In an automotive driver-assistance system, diagnostic trouble codes and hardware watchdog timers expose hardware or communication faults, yet they do not measure how often a perception model detects pedestrians."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "Pay attention to: silent degradation vs loud failures; degradation equation and engineering levers (initial accuracy, sensitivity, drift); training-serving skew and operational monitoring."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Why does the degradation equation indicate that tracking statistical data drift ($\\mathcal{D}(P_t \\lVert P_0)$) alone is necessary but not sufficient to determine whether a deployed model must be retrained?", "md": "Statistical divergence ($\\mathcal{D}(P_t \\lVert P_0)$) indicates that the input distribution has shifted, but divergence alone does not dictate whether prediction accuracy has actually dropped or by how much. Determining whether retraining is necessary requires monitoring labeled ground-truth outcomes or calibrated business proxies alongside drift metrics to confirm whether the shift has caused meaningful performance degradation."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-01-06-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "In the degradation equation $\\text{Accuracy}(t) \\approx \\text{Accuracy}_0 - \\lambda \\cdot \\mathcal{D}(P_t \\lVert P_0)$, what do the terms $\\mathcal{D}(P_t \\lVert P_0)$ and $\\lambda$ represent, and which engineering lever addresses $\\lambda$?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. In the degradation equation, $\\mathcal{D}(P_t \\lVert P_0)$ measures statistical divergence (such as KL divergence or Wasserstein distance) between the current operational data distribution $P_t$ and the baseline training distribution $P_0$, while $\\lambda$ represents the model's sensitivity to that shift. The engineering lever for $\\lambda$ is making the model more robust to shift through domain generalization, data augmentation, and regularized training, which flattens the degradation slope. Explanations referring to clock jitter, memory bandwidth ratios, or learning rate schedules confuse statistical data drift with hardware execution or optimization hyperparameters.", "type": "single", "options": ["$\\mathcal{D}(P_t \\lVert P_0)$ is hardware clock jitter, $\\lambda$ is GPU temperature sensitivity, and it is addressed by dynamic voltage and frequency scaling.", "$\\mathcal{D}(P_t \\lVert P_0)$ is statistical divergence between live production data and training data, $\\lambda$ is model sensitivity to distribution shift, and it is addressed by robust training and domain adaptation to flatten the degradation curve.", "$\\mathcal{D}(P_t \\lVert P_0)$ is the memory bandwidth ratio, $\\lambda$ is cache miss penalty, and it is addressed by prefetching weights into on-chip memory.", "$\\mathcal{D}(P_t \\lVert P_0)$ is training loss divergence, $\\lambda$ is the learning rate decay, and it is addressed by tuning the optimization algorithm."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the mathematical terms of the degradation equation and map them to their corresponding engineering interventions."]}, {"q": "A production fraud detection model begins misclassifying high-risk transactions immediately after deployment. An audit reveals that the training pipeline extracted user account age in integer days, while the live inference microservice computed account age in fractional floating-point seconds. What type of systems failure does this scenario illustrate?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. This is a classic example of training-serving skew: the mathematical representation of a feature (account age in days vs. seconds) differed between the offline training environment and the online serving path. Both pipelines executed without software exceptions, yet the model received inputs outside its learned numerical distribution, causing silent prediction degradation. It is not hardware corruption, deserialization latency, or multi-year macroeconomic drift.", "type": "single", "options": ["Training-serving skew, where discrepancies in feature computation between training and serving pipelines cause silent model degradation despite bug-free code execution.", "Hardware memory corruption caused by unaligned tensor strides in the GPU inference runtime.", "Unbounded latency growth where deserialization overhead violates the service-level agreement.", "Concept drift caused by macroeconomic shifts in consumer purchasing behavior over multiple years."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: identify and diagnose training-serving skew as a structural cause of silent degradation in ML systems."]}, {"q": "Holding drift sensitivity fixed, improving the initial training accuracy ($\\text{Accuracy}_0$) of an ML model shifts the starting point of the degradation curve upward, but does not change the model's rate of accuracy decline ($\\lambda$) with respect to distribution drift over time.", "explanation": "True. Under the fixed-drift-sensitivity assumption in the degradation equation ($\\text{Accuracy}(t) \\approx \\text{Accuracy}_0 - \\lambda \\cdot \\mathcal{D}(P_t \\lVert P_0)$), increasing $\\text{Accuracy}_0$ raises the baseline intercept, while the rate of decline is still governed by $\\lambda$. Better models can also change sensitivity, but this statement holds only when $\\lambda$ is assumed fixed.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": true, "hints": ["Think about: distinguish between baseline accuracy improvements and distribution shift sensitivity in ML model degradation."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze the mathematical terms of the degradation equation and map them to their corresponding engineering interventions.", "You can now identify and diagnose training-serving skew as a structural cause of silent degradation in ML systems.", "You can now explain why drift monitoring must be paired with outcome evaluation to justify model retraining decisions.", "You can now distinguish between baseline accuracy improvements and distribution shift sensitivity in ML model degradation."]}], "id": "v1-01-06-when-ml-systems-fail-silently", "chapter": "v1-01", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-01-07-iron-law-of-ml-systems": {"title": "Iron Law of ML Systems", "summary": "Apply the Iron Law of ML Systems to compare memory-bandwidth-bound and compute-bound workloads.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-01", "sections": ["Iron Law of ML Systems", "Lighthouse models put the iron law into practice"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Iron Law of ML Systems** in *Introduction* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Apply the Iron Law of ML Systems to compare memory-bandwidth-bound and compute-bound workloads\n- Calculate the pipelined critical-path lower bound of the Iron Law under overlapped data movement and compute\n- Explain the physical basis of the data-movement energy tax and identify hardware/software techniques to reduce it\n- Calculate all iron-law terms and choose an intervention under sequential and ideal-overlap execution assumptions"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Introduction, § Iron Law of ML Systems", "md": "Computational scale has a physical cost, and it shows up as two familiar failures. A training job stalls when storage cannot feed an accelerator; an inference path misses its deadline when model state moves too slowly through memory or across the network. Their symptoms differ, but both are governed by how the three D·A·M axes combine to consume wall-clock time."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Lighthouse models put the iron law into practice\n\nPay attention to: Iron Law equation terms (Data, Compute, Latency); pipelined critical-path lower bound (max formulation); energy tax and data movement vs arithmetic ratio; Return on Compute (RoC) and Lighthouse Models."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Based on the energy cost model $E_{\\text{total}} \\approx D_{\\text{vol}} \\times E_{\\text{move}} + O \\times E_{\\text{compute}}$, explain why moving a byte from off-chip DRAM costs roughly 145 times more energy than an FP16 arithmetic operation, and state one system optimization that mitigates this energy tax.", "md": "Data movement requires charging and discharging physical capacitive wires across millimeters of silicon and printed circuit board traces to off-chip DRAM, whereas arithmetic operations occur locally within microscopic on-chip arithmetic circuits. Optimizations that mitigate this tax include operator fusion, weight quantization (e.g., INT8/INT4 to reduce $D_{\\text{vol}}$), and tiling data to maximize reuse in local on-chip SRAM caches."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-01-07-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "In the Iron Law of ML Systems, $T = \\frac{D_{\\text{vol}}}{\\text{BW}} + \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}} + L_{\\text{lat}}$, how do the terms differ when analyzing small-batch autoregressive LLM token decode versus large-batch ResNet-50 image inference?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. In small-batch autoregressive decode, a language model must stream its entire weight footprint and cached context from memory to produce each single token, resulting in low arithmetic intensity where memory bandwidth ($\\text{BW}$) limits execution time ($D_{\\text{vol}}/\\text{BW}$). In contrast, batched convolutional networks like ResNet-50 repeatedly reuse filter weights across pixels and batch elements, amortizing memory transfers and making arithmetic throughput ($\\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}}$) the limiting constraint. The other options misidentify the limiting physical terms or misattribute DLRM's embedding table capacity constraint to ResNet-50.", "type": "single", "options": ["LLM decode is dominated by the latency term $L_{\\text{lat}}$, while ResNet-50 is dominated by the data movement term $D_{\\text{vol}}/\\text{BW}$.", "Both workloads are dominated strictly by the compute term $\\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}}$, making memory bandwidth irrelevant.", "ResNet-50 is memory-capacity bound by embedding tables, while LLM decode is bound by network serialization overhead.", "Small-batch LLM decode is bound by the data movement term ($D_{\\text{vol}}/\\text{BW}$) because billions of weights and cached context must be fetched from memory for every single token generated, whereas batched ResNet-50 reuses weight parameters across many inputs and spatial locations, making the compute term ($\\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}}$) dominant."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: apply the Iron Law of ML Systems to compare memory-bandwidth-bound and compute-bound workloads."]}, {"q": "When data transfers and arithmetic are overlapped in a pipelined ML runtime, so that the next block of data loads while the current block is computed, how is the sequential additive Iron Law modified, and what determines execution time?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. When data transfers and compute execute concurrently in an overlapped pipeline, the execution time is governed by the critical path: $T_{\\text{pipelined}} \\ge \\max\\left(\\frac{D_{\\text{vol}}}{\\text{BW}}, \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}}\\right) + L_{\\text{lat}}$. The slower stage bounds performance while completely or partially hiding the latency of the faster stage (assuming $L_{\\text{lat}}$ represents non-overlapped orchestration overhead). Multiplicative forms, min formulations, and adding bytes directly to FLOPs in the numerator violate physical laws and dimensional consistency.", "type": "single", "options": ["$T_{\\text{pipelined}} = \\frac{D_{\\text{vol}}}{\\text{BW}} \\times \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}} \\times L_{\\text{lat}}$", "$T_{\\text{pipelined}} = \\min\\left(\\frac{D_{\\text{vol}}}{\\text{BW}}, \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}}\\right) + L_{\\text{lat}}$", "$T_{\\text{pipelined}} \\ge \\max\\left(\\frac{D_{\\text{vol}}}{\\text{BW}}, \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}}\\right) + L_{\\text{lat}}$, where the slower pipeline stage dictates the critical path while hiding the latency of the faster stage.", "$T_{\\text{pipelined}} = \\frac{D_{\\text{vol}} + O}{\\text{BW} + R_{\\text{peak}}} + L_{\\text{lat}}$"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the pipelined critical-path lower bound of the Iron Law under overlapped data movement and compute."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: A hypothetical sequential workload moves 1 GB at 100 GB/s, performs 2 GFLOPs at an effective rate of 100 GFLOP/s, and incurs 5 ms of fixed overhead. Calculate its execution time. Which single upgrade gives the larger reduction: doubling bandwidth or doubling the effective arithmetic rate? What is the ideal-overlap lower bound before either upgrade if overhead remains outside the overlapping phases?", "md": "Movement takes 10 ms, computation takes 20 ms, and overhead adds 5 ms, giving 35 ms sequential time. Doubling bandwidth reduces movement to 5 ms and the total to 30 ms. Doubling effective arithmetic rate reduces computation to 10 ms and the total to 25 ms, so it gives the larger reduction. Ideal overlap yields a lower bound of max(10 ms, 20 ms) + 5 ms = 25 ms before either upgrade; equality requires the stated ideal overlap. The effective arithmetic rate already includes hardware efficiency."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now apply the Iron Law of ML Systems to compare memory-bandwidth-bound and compute-bound workloads.", "You can now calculate the pipelined critical-path lower bound of the Iron Law under overlapped data movement and compute.", "You can now explain the physical basis of the data-movement energy tax and identify hardware/software techniques to reduce it.", "You can now calculate all iron-law terms and choose an intervention under sequential and ideal-overlap execution assumptions."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Execution time", "def": "Time spent moving data, performing arithmetic, and paying system overhead; the applicable overlap model depends on the execution boundary."}], "id": "v1-01-07-iron-law-of-ml-systems", "chapter": "v1-01", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-01-07b-lab-iron-law": {"title": "Lab: put numbers into the iron law", "summary": "Compute the three iron-law terms for one workload, find the dominant one and predict what an upgrade buys.", "minutes": 25, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "The previous lesson gave the iron law in words. Here you use it the way an engineer does: plug in a workload, see which term is largest, and predict what a faster part would actually buy you.\n\nThe workload for the whole lab: one accelerator step that **moves 8 GB** of weights and activations over a **2,000 GB/s** memory bus, performs **$10^{11}$ FLOPs** at an effective **100 TFLOP/s** (peak 250 TFLOP/s at 40% utilisation), and pays **0.5 ms** of fixed overhead."}, {"type": "derivation", "title": "Sequential time, term by term", "rel": "=", "steps": [{"lhs": "T", "rhs": "\\frac{D_{vol}}{BW} + \\frac{O}{R_{peak}\\,\\eta_{hw}} + L_{lat}", "why": "The iron law with the three costs paid one after the other."}, {"lhs": "\\frac{D_{vol}}{BW}", "rhs": "\\frac{8\\times10^{9}\\ \\text{B}}{2\\times10^{12}\\ \\text{B/s}} = 4\\ \\text{ms}", "why": "Bytes divided by bytes per second leaves seconds."}, {"lhs": "\\frac{O}{R_{peak}\\,\\eta_{hw}}", "rhs": "\\frac{10^{11}}{250\\times10^{12}\\times0.4} = 1\\ \\text{ms}", "why": "The effective rate already includes utilisation; do not multiply by it twice."}, {"lhs": "T", "rhs": "4 + 1 + 0.5 = 5.5\\ \\text{ms}", "why": "Movement is the largest term, so this step is memory-bound."}]}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "key", "md": "Doubling peak compute would shrink only the 1 ms term. Doubling bandwidth shrinks the 4 ms term. The iron law tells you which upgrade to buy **before** you buy it."}, {"type": "widget", "id": "v1-01-lab-iron-widget", "widget": "iron-law", "title": "Try it: the same workload in the calculator", "md": "The calculator starts at the workload above, with the stages run one after the other. Move the bandwidth slider and watch which term leads.", "params": {"ops": 100000000000.0, "peak": 250, "eta": 0.4, "dvol": 8000000000.0, "bw": 2000, "lat": 0.0005, "pipelined": 0}, "challenge": {"md": "Set bandwidth to **4,000 GB/s** (twice as fast). What is the new total time, in milliseconds?", "answer": 3.5, "tolerance": 0.05, "unit": "ms", "hint": "Only the movement term changes: 8 GB at 4,000 GB/s.", "explanation": "Movement drops from 4 ms to 2 ms; compute (1 ms) and overhead (0.5 ms) stay. 2 + 1 + 0.5 = 3.5 ms. Twice the bandwidth gave 1.57x, not 2x."}}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "If movement and compute overlap perfectly (the pipelined bound), what is the best possible time before and after the bandwidth upgrade?", "md": "Before: max(4, 1) + 0.5 = **4.5 ms**. After: max(2, 1) + 0.5 = **2.5 ms**. After the upgrade movement is still the slower stage; once it falls below 1 ms, more bandwidth stops helping and compute becomes the wall."}, {"type": "playground", "id": "v1-01-lab-iron-play", "title": "Write the iron law as a function", "md": "Complete `iron_law`. With `overlap=False` it returns the sum of the three terms; with `overlap=True` it returns the pipelined bound `max(movement, compute) + overhead`. Units: bytes, bytes/s, FLOPs, FLOP/s, seconds. **Run and check** tests it on the lab's workload.", "code": "def iron_law(d_vol, bw, ops, peak, eta, lat, overlap=False):\n    movement = d_vol / bw\n    compute = None  # TODO: operations divided by the effective rate\n    # TODO: return the sequential sum or the pipelined bound\n    return None\n\n\nt = iron_law(8e9, 2e12, 1e11, 250e12, 0.4, 0.5e-3)\nprint(\"sequential:\", t)\n", "check": "assert abs(iron_law(8e9, 2e12, 1e11, 250e12, 0.4, 0.5e-3) - 5.5e-3) < 1e-9, 'sequential time should be 5.5 ms'\nassert abs(iron_law(8e9, 2e12, 1e11, 250e12, 0.4, 0.5e-3, overlap=True) - 4.5e-3) < 1e-9, 'pipelined bound should be 4.5 ms'\nassert abs(iron_law(8e9, 4e12, 1e11, 250e12, 0.4, 0.5e-3) - 3.5e-3) < 1e-9, 'with 4 TB/s the sum is 3.5 ms'", "hint": "`compute = ops / (peak * eta)`; then `return (max(movement, compute) if overlap else movement + compute) + lat`.", "solution": "def iron_law(d_vol, bw, ops, peak, eta, lat, overlap=False):\n    movement = d_vol / bw\n    compute = ops / (peak * eta)\n    core = max(movement, compute) if overlap else movement + compute\n    return core + lat\n\n\nprint(\"sequential:\", iron_law(8e9, 2e12, 1e11, 250e12, 0.4, 0.5e-3))\nprint(\"pipelined: \", iron_law(8e9, 2e12, 1e11, 250e12, 0.4, 0.5e-3, overlap=True))\n"}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-01-lab-iron-quiz", "title": "Check your numbers", "questions": [{"type": "numeric", "q": "A dense language model with $N = 10^9$ parameters trains on $D = 2\\times10^{10}$ tokens. Using $O \\approx 6ND$, how many FLOPs is that, in units of $10^{20}$?", "answer": 1.2, "tolerance": 0.01, "unit": "× 10²⁰ FLOP", "explanation": "6 × 1 × 2 = 12, and 10⁹ × 10¹⁰ = 10¹⁹, so 12 × 10¹⁹ = 1.2 × 10²⁰ FLOP.", "hints": ["6 × 10⁹ × 2 × 10¹⁰."]}, {"type": "numeric", "q": "Generating one token with a 7-billion-parameter model costs about $2N$ FLOPs. How many GFLOPs per token?", "answer": 14, "tolerance": 0.1, "unit": "GFLOP", "explanation": "2N = 1.4 × 10¹⁰ FLOP = 14 GFLOP per token, forward pass only.", "hints": ["2 × 7 × 10⁹."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "In the lab's workload, which change shortens the sequential time the most?", "options": ["Doubling peak compute", "Doubling memory bandwidth", "Halving the fixed overhead", "All three save the same time"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Doubling bandwidth removes 2 ms, doubling compute removes 0.5 ms, halving overhead removes 0.25 ms. Fix the largest term first.", "hints": ["Compare how many milliseconds each change removes."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["The iron law adds movement, compute and overhead; the largest term sets the pace.", "An upgrade only helps through the term it shrinks, so it never speeds up the whole by its own factor.", "With perfect overlap the bound is max(movement, compute) + overhead."]}], "terms": [{"term": "pipelined bound", "def": "max(movement time, compute time) + overhead: the best case when data movement and computation overlap perfectly"}], "source": {"chapter": "v1-01", "sections": ["hands-on lab"]}, "id": "v1-01-07b-lab-iron-law", "chapter": "v1-01", "kind": "lesson"}, "v1-01-08-three-dimensions-of-ml-efficienc": {"title": "Three Dimensions of ML Efficiency", "summary": "Analyze the interaction between algorithmic efficiency gains and aggregate compute growth through Jevons paradox.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-01", "sections": ["Three Dimensions of ML Efficiency", "Return on compute: Weighing accuracy against cost"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Three Dimensions of ML Efficiency** in *Introduction* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze the interaction between algorithmic efficiency gains and aggregate compute growth through Jevons paradox\n- Evaluate the divergence between training-compute growth and Moore's law scaling\n- Compare the three dimensions of ML efficiency and justify their pedagogical build order\n- Interpret what the algorithmic-efficiency measurement does and does not show about hardware progress\n- Identify the definition and term for Return on Compute (RoC)"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Introduction, § Three Dimensions of ML Efficiency", "md": "Because training compute grew faster than hardware (a later section), reaching a given capability had to cost less on every factor of a later section. AlexNet's 2012 run (a later section) is where that pressure began. Its two consumer GPUs, running for about six days on the … labeled images of the 2012 ImageNet challenge training split, made up a budget a university laboratory could afford. Modern foundation models, large models trained on broad data and then adapted to many tasks, demand training compute roughly … orders of magnitude larger."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Return on compute: Weighing accuracy against cost\n\nPay attention to: three efficiency dimensions (Algorithmic, Compute, Data Selection); historical 44x algorithmic efficiency gain vs \\$10^8 training compute growth; training-compute growth (3.4mo doubling) versus Moore's law (24mo doubling); Jevons paradox / Efficiency paradox in ML systems."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Name the three dimensions of ML efficiency described in the chapter and explain how the pedagogical order in which they are taught (Data Selection -> Model Compression -> Hardware Acceleration) differs from a prescribed optimization sequence.", "md": "The three dimensions are data selection, algorithmic efficiency through model compression, and compute efficiency through hardware acceleration. The chapter teaches them in that order as a pedagogical sequence: first decide which examples merit computation, then reduce the model work and storage the task requires, and only then study how the remaining work maps efficiently onto hardware. In practice, this is not a rigid historical or operational timeline, because the right intervention follows the diagnosed limiting constraint and hardware limits can shape data and model choices from the start."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-01-08-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Between 2012 (AlexNet) and 2019 (EfficientNet), algorithmic efficiency for ImageNet classification improved by approximately 44× for a fixed accuracy target. Separately, the chapter uses AlexNet-to-GPT-scale growth as an illustrative endpoint comparison showing that training compute expanded by roughly \\$10^8×. How can the 'efficiency paradox' (Jevons paradox in ML systems) reconcile these two observations?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The efficiency paradox explains that improving algorithmic efficiency lowers the compute needed to hit a fixed target, which makes additional scale more affordable rather than unnecessary. Organizations can reinvest those savings into larger models and broader datasets to pursue higher capabilities. The endpoint compute-growth comparison is illustrative, not a historical doubling-rate fit, so the key contrast is fixed-target efficiency gain versus expanded training ambition. Explanations based on inference-only metrics, deliberate hardware throttling, or quantization artifacts misread the chapter's argument.", "type": "single", "options": ["Efficiency lowers the compute needed for a fixed accuracy, and organizations can reinvest the savings in larger models or more experiments, although the plotted trends alone do not establish that they did.", "Algorithmic efficiency metrics only apply to inference workloads, while training compute growth applies exclusively to cloud data centers.", "Hardware manufacturers deliberately slowed down clock frequencies to increase total data center power consumption.", "The 44$\\times$ algorithmic gain was an artifact of integer quantization that could not be replicated in 16-bit floating-point training."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the interaction between algorithmic efficiency gains and aggregate compute growth through Jevons paradox."]}, {"q": "The chapter contrasts a 3.4-month doubling in training compute (2012–2018) with the roughly two-year doubling associated with Moore's law. Why does this divergence motivate efficiency work?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Training compute grew faster than the roughly two-year doubling associated with Moore's law, motivating efficiency improvements across algorithms, software runtimes, and hardware. Because transistor scaling alone cannot keep pace with that growth, data selection, model compression, and hardware acceleration all help keep useful systems within reach.", "type": "single", "options": ["The latency gap between CPU cache access and local register access in accelerator memory hierarchies.", "The widening divergence between the rate at which frontier AI model compute demand has grown (doubling roughly every 3.4 months) and the rate at which semiconductor physics advances hardware density via Moore's Law (doubling roughly every 24 months).", "The difference in training loss between supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning from human feedback.", "The discrepancy between open-source framework code and proprietary GPU driver implementations."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the divergence between training-compute growth and Moore's law scaling."]}, {"q": "The 44$\\times$ reduction in training compute from AlexNet to EfficientNet can be directly compared with Moore's-law transistor growth to show that algorithms outpaced hardware.", "explanation": "False. Hernandez and Brown counted operations at fixed accuracy, so the figure measures algorithmic progress only. Moore's law describes transistor integration, which does not by itself determine useful throughput or energy efficiency, so the two are not a matched comparison.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: interpret what the algorithmic-efficiency measurement does and does not show about hardware progress."]}, {"q": "In economic analysis of ML systems, the quantitative metric that measures the incremental gain in model accuracy achieved per added dollar of infrastructure investment is called the ____.", "explanation": "return on compute. It measures the incremental accuracy gained per added dollar of compute cost.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["return on compute"], "hints": ["Think about: identify the definition and term for Return on Compute (RoC)."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze the interaction between algorithmic efficiency gains and aggregate compute growth through Jevons paradox.", "You can now evaluate the divergence between training-compute growth and Moore's law scaling.", "You can now compare the three dimensions of ML efficiency and justify their pedagogical build order.", "You can now interpret what the algorithmic-efficiency measurement does and does not show about hardware progress."]}], "id": "v1-01-08-three-dimensions-of-ml-efficienc", "chapter": "v1-01", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-01-09-ai-engineering-as-a-discipline": {"title": "AI Engineering as a Discipline", "summary": "Analyze the feedback loops of the ML system lifecycle and explain how they manage degradation.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-01", "sections": ["AI Engineering as a Discipline", "Engineering across the ML lifecycle", "Deployment context shapes the lifecycle"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **AI Engineering as a Discipline** in *Introduction* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze the feedback loops of the ML system lifecycle and explain how they manage degradation\n- Explain why benchmark accuracy does not carry over to deployment populations that differ from evaluation data\n- Classify the sequential stages of the ML system lifecycle\n- Explain the disciplinary emergence and core mandate of AI engineering\n- Quantify the multi-order-of-magnitude memory and compute span across cloud, edge, mobile, and TinyML deployment paradigms"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Introduction, § AI Engineering as a Discipline", "md": "A cloud service may optimize throughput across racks of accelerators, while an edge device must remain within a strict battery and thermal envelope, so the same model can be efficient in one setting and unusable in another. An engineer who must hold predictive quality and resource cost to one specification across such different operating budgets needs to combine three quantitative tools."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Engineering across the ML lifecycle\n- Deployment context shapes the lifecycle\n\nPay attention to: AI engineering definition (stochastic behavior under deterministic reliability); six-stage ML lifecycle and two core feedback loops; deployment paradigms and production case studies (Waymo, FarmBeats, AlphaFold)."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: The chapter compares AI engineering with the earlier emergence of computer engineering. What do the two disciplines share, and what does AI engineering add?", "md": "Both combine methods from established disciplines around a common set of requirements: computer engineering joined digital circuits, processor design, and software, so that moving a function between hardware and software could be judged by speed, power, and flexibility. AI engineering extends that shared design problem to learned behavior and the data that shapes it. Its requirements can be fixed, such as a recall target over a stated population, while the evidence that a system meets them stays statistical and must be reassessed as operating conditions change."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-01-09-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "The ML project responsibilities run from Problem Definition through Data Collection & Preparation, Model Development & Training, Evaluation & Validation, Deployment & Integration, and Monitoring & Maintenance. Which feedback pattern correctly interprets the finer-grained pipeline shown in the chapter?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Failed evaluation and production warnings initiate investigation against the requirements established during Problem Definition. A data coverage problem can return work to collection or preparation; a pipeline defect may need a software repair, and a model limitation may need a model update. Drift alone does not establish quality loss or select retraining. Any selected update passes through evaluation before redeployment. The figure separates collection from preparation to show pipeline detail, while the six project responsibilities include the upstream task definition.", "type": "single", "options": ["Deployment returns to Training on compiler warnings, and Collection returns to Preparation on memory leaks.", "Monitoring returns to Deployment on network timeouts, and Preparation returns to Collection on syntax errors.", "Evaluation can return work to preparation when investigation identifies a data problem, and monitoring can motivate new collection when outcome evidence and diagnosis identify a coverage gap.", "Model Training returns to Hardware Design on arithmetic overflow, and Deployment returns to Operating System Kernel on driver faults."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the feedback loops of the ML system lifecycle and explain how they manage degradation."]}, {"q": "The chapter uses autonomous driving to show a challenge that production ML systems share. What does the Waymo Open Dataset's report of domain gaps among San Francisco, Phoenix, and Mountain View imply for engineering such a system?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The gaps show that evaluation inputs and deployment inputs can differ, and a model's measured accuracy holds only for the population it was evaluated on. Where perception runs does not change what inputs the model sees. Retraining from scratch per city is one possible remedy, not what the evidence implies, and the gaps reflect real differences in driving environments rather than labeling mistakes.", "type": "single", "options": ["A model that scores well on inputs from one setting can lose accuracy where deployment inputs differ, so every remote update must be revalidated against the population it will meet.", "Domain gaps disappear once perception runs on the vehicle rather than in the cloud, because local execution removes network effects.", "Domain gaps show that a perception model must be retrained from scratch for each city before any deployment is possible.", "Domain gaps are labeling errors that a larger annotation budget eliminates, after which one evaluation covers every city."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: explain why benchmark accuracy does not carry over to deployment populations that differ from evaluation data."]}, {"q": "Place the six ML project responsibilities in their initial progression from defining the task to maintaining the deployed system:", "explanation": "The correct order is (4) -> (5) -> (2) -> (1) -> (6) -> (3).\n- (4) Problem Definition: Specify the task, operating population, constraints, and acceptance requirements.\n- (5) Data Collection & Preparation: Gather representative evidence and construct the inputs used for learning.\n- (2) Model Development & Training: Choose and train a model within the resource budget.\n- (1) Evaluation & Validation: Assess task quality and system requirements under a stated protocol.\n- (6) Deployment & Integration: Integrate the validated artifact into its operating environment.\n- (3) Monitoring & Maintenance: Gather production evidence and diagnose whether earlier work needs revision.\nThis is an initial progression, not a rule that every alert repeats every stage.", "type": "order", "items": ["Problem Definition", "Data Collection & Preparation", "Model Development & Training", "Evaluation & Validation", "Deployment & Integration", "Monitoring & Maintenance"], "hints": ["Think about: classify the sequential stages of the ML system lifecycle."]}, {"q": "Across the four deployment paradigms in the chapter (Cloud, Edge, Mobile, TinyML), approximately what orders-of-magnitude span exists between the highest tier (Cloud) and the lowest tier (TinyML) in memory capacity and compute throughput?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. The deployment spectrum spans approximately five orders of magnitude (\\$10^5\\times$) in memory capacity (from $\\approx 10^{11}\\text{ bytes}$ in cloud accelerator nodes down to $\\approx 10^6\\text{ bytes}$ in TinyML microcontrollers) and six orders of magnitude (\\$10^6\\times$) in compute throughput (from $\\approx 10^{15}\\text{ ops/s}$ in cloud down to $\\approx 10^9\\text{ ops/s}$ in TinyML). This divergence of five to six orders of magnitude prevents arbitrary model transfer across tiers. Compression or redesign may be needed, and the resulting model must satisfy the target memory, timing, energy, and quality requirements. Spans of \\$10^2$ or \\$10^3$ drastically underestimate the divergence between cloud data centers and microcontrollers, while spans of \\$10^{12}$ to \\$10^{15}$ exceed physical realities.", "type": "single", "options": ["\\$10^2$ (100$\\times$) span in memory capacity and \\$10^3$ (1,000$\\times$) span in compute throughput", "\\$10^3$ (1,000$\\times$) span in memory capacity and \\$10^4$ (10,000$\\times$) span in compute throughput", "\\$10^{12}$ (one trillion$\\times$) span in memory capacity and \\$10^{15}$ span in compute throughput", "\\$10^5$ (100,000$\\times$) span in memory capacity and \\$10^6$ (one million$\\times$) span in compute throughput"], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: quantify the multi-order-of-magnitude memory and compute span across cloud, edge, mobile, and TinyML deployment paradigms."]}, {"q": "A team designing a battery-powered smart doorbell vision system chooses a TinyML microcontroller node over a cloud-offloaded architecture. What primary constraint tradeoff drove this architectural decision?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The stated design prioritizes battery operation, local privacy, and independence from intermittent connectivity, accepting a small memory envelope. Local execution avoids sending the visual inputs needed for remote inference, but the chosen model must still fit and meet its timing and energy budgets. Cloud execution is computationally feasible; its complete cost includes radio transfers, network delay, and remote inference, with transfer energy depending on frame volume and duty cycle. Microcontrollers still require dataset curation and evaluation.", "type": "single", "options": ["The doorbell must operate under a strict milliwatt power envelope on battery while preserving user visual privacy and avoiding reliance on intermittent wireless connectivity, accepting severe kilobyte-scale memory limits.", "TinyML microcontrollers provide higher FP16 peak FLOP/s throughput than multi-GPU cloud nodes.", "Cloud-based serving architectures cannot support visual wake-word classification algorithms.", "Microcontrollers eliminate the need for dataset annotation and model evaluation."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the constraint trade-offs governing TinyML microcontroller deployments versus cloud architectures."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze the feedback loops of the ML system lifecycle and explain how they manage degradation.", "You can now explain why benchmark accuracy does not carry over to deployment populations that differ from evaluation data.", "You can now classify the sequential stages of the ML system lifecycle.", "You can now explain the disciplinary emergence and core mandate of AI engineering."]}], "id": "v1-01-09-ai-engineering-as-a-discipline", "chapter": "v1-01", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-01-10-book-organization": {"title": "Book Organization", "summary": "Explain the pedagogical progression and architectural logic of the textbook's four parts.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-01", "sections": ["Book Organization"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Book Organization** in *Introduction* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Explain the pedagogical progression and architectural logic of the textbook's four parts\n- Distinguish between the physical constraints of the single-node regime and fleet-scale cluster orchestration\n- Identify the dependency ordering between model architecture fundamentals and performance optimization techniques"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Introduction, § Book Organization", "md": "Production ML work divides into responsibilities that engineering teams own: data engineering, training systems, deployment infrastructure, operations and monitoring, and ethics and governance. Those boundaries leak. Upstream data pipelines limit what training can reach, training choices fix the memory and latency footprint at serving time, and governance constrains every stage, so the book does not follow an organization chart. Its four parts follow the order in which constraints must be understood. The organizing principle is *context before theory*."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "Pay attention to: four-part book structure (Foundations, Build, Optimize, Deploy); context before theory pedagogical principle; single-node vs fleet-scale orchestration scope."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: What distinguishes the single-node execution regime covered in this volume from the fleet-scale orchestration regime addressed in advanced distributed systems?", "md": "The single-node regime focuses on one host with 1 to 8 accelerators coordinating over high-speed on-node interconnects and local device memory (where bottlenecks include memory bandwidth, capacity, and compute throughput), whereas fleet scale coordinates thousands of nodes across data center networks where bisection bandwidth and cluster-wide network fabrics become the bottleneck."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-01-10-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "What is the primary pedagogical rationale behind organizing the textbook into the four sequential parts: Part I (Foundations of ML Systems), Part II (Development and Training), Part III (Optimization and Acceleration), and Part IV (Deployment and Operations)?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The organizing principle is 'context before theory': establishing the physical constraints, deployment tiers, and diagnostic vocabulary in Part I (Foundations of ML Systems) provides the mental model needed before constructing models in Part II (Development and Training), tuning their arithmetic and memory efficiency in Part III (Optimization and Acceleration), and managing their lifecycle in Part IV (Deployment and Operations).", "type": "single", "options": ["To teach low-level CUDA kernel programming before introducing high-level machine learning concepts.", "To ensure students deploy production systems in the cloud before learning how neural networks compute predictions.", "To establish the systems landscape, constraints, and vocabulary (context before theory) before constructing models, optimizing their physical execution, and managing them in production.", "To separate data science students who only read Part II from hardware engineering students who only read Part III."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: explain the pedagogical progression and architectural logic of the textbook's four parts."]}, {"q": "In the textbook's pedagogical build order, model compression and hardware acceleration (Part III) are introduced before neural computation and network architectures (Part II).", "explanation": "False. The curriculum follows 'context before theory': neural computation and network architectures are developed in Part II (Development and Training) to establish model mechanisms before Part III (Optimization and Acceleration) explores techniques like quantization, pruning, and hardware acceleration to optimize their execution.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: identify the dependency ordering between model architecture fundamentals and performance optimization techniques."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now explain the pedagogical progression and architectural logic of the textbook's four parts.", "You can now distinguish between the physical constraints of the single-node regime and fleet-scale cluster orchestration.", "You can now identify the dependency ordering between model architecture fundamentals and performance optimization techniques."]}], "id": "v1-01-10-book-organization", "chapter": "v1-01", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-01-11-fallacies-and-pitfalls": {"title": "Fallacies and Pitfalls", "summary": "Calculate end-to-end pipeline speedup under Amdahl's Law when optimizing individual ML system components.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-01", "sections": ["Fallacies and Pitfalls", "Summary", "Summary"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "This lesson follows **Fallacies and Pitfalls** in *Introduction* (Vol I). By the end you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate end-to-end pipeline speedup under Amdahl's Law when optimizing individual ML system components\n- Analyze the fallacies of relying exclusively on benchmark accuracy to assess production readiness\n- Explain why traditional CI/CD pipelines cannot detect silent degradation in deployed ML systems\n- Evaluate model selection trade-offs between incremental accuracy gains and production execution budgets"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, Introduction, § Fallacies and Pitfalls", "md": "Every chapter in this book closes with a section like this one. A **fallacy** is a belief that sounds right but is false, a **pitfall** is a practice that is easy to fall into and costly to recover from, and each entry corrects the mistake with evidence from the chapter."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "Map of the section", "md": "The section is organised as:\n\n- Summary\n\nPay attention to: Amdahl's law pitfall in ML inference pipelines; benchmark fallacy vs production distribution shift; deploy once fallacy and silent drift; algorithmic superiority fallacy vs latency budgets."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Why can code and infrastructure checks in a standard CI/CD pipeline fail to detect a loss of predictive quality, and how should drift and outcome monitoring guide the response?", "md": "Compilation, unit tests, and container health can remain healthy while the operating population changes and predictive quality falls. Drift monitoring warns that the input distribution has changed; outcome measurements establish whether relevant quality has declined. Engineers then diagnose the cause and select a repair, which may involve the pipeline, data, model, or deployment requirements. Neither degradation nor retraining follows inevitably from a drift signal."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-01-11-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "An inference pipeline consists of three sequential stages: data preprocessing taking 60 ms, model inference taking 45 ms, and output postprocessing taking 25 ms (total latency = 130 ms). An engineering team applies kernel fusion and quantization to achieve a \\$3× speedup on the model inference stage alone (reducing it from 45 ms to 15 ms). What is the resulting end-to-end pipeline latency and approximate overall system speedup, and what principle does this demonstrate?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Total initial time is \\$60 + 45 + 25 = 130\\text{ ms}$. With a \\$3\\times$ speedup on inference alone (\\$45 / 3 = 15\\text{ ms}$), the new total latency is \\$60 + 15 + 25 = 100\\text{ ms}$. The overall speedup is \\$130 / 100 = 1.30\\times$, representing a 23% overall latency reduction $(1 - 1/1.30 = 0.231)$. This is a classic demonstration of Amdahl's Law: because the inference component accounted for only \\$45/130 \\approx 34.6\\%$ of total execution time, even a dramatic \\$3\\times$ component improvement yields a modest 23% end-to-end gain. Assuming a \\$3\\times$ overall pipeline speedup commits the pitfall of ignoring system interactions.", "type": "single", "options": ["New latency is 100 ms (an overall speedup of $\\approx 1.30\\times$, or a 23% reduction in execution time), illustrating Amdahl's Law that component-level speedups yield only marginal end-to-end gains when non-optimized stages dominate.", "New latency is 43.3 ms (a \\$3.0× overall speedup, or 67% reduction), illustrating linear speedup scaling across modular microservices.", "New latency is 15 ms, illustrating that hardware acceleration bypasses pre- and post-processing stages.", "New latency is 115 ms, illustrating that quantization overhead cancels out inference gains."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: calculate end-to-end pipeline speedup under Amdahl's Law when optimizing individual ML system components."]}, {"q": "Why does high accuracy on curated benchmark datasets (such as ImageNet or GLUE) frequently fail to guarantee production readiness in real-world deployments?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Curated benchmarks evaluate accuracy on fixed, preprocessed test distributions in unconstrained compute environments. Production deployments encounter domain shifts, slang, sensor noise, demographic variations, strict real-time latency budgets, and hardware precision/memory constraints that offline benchmarks never capture. Explanations regarding CPU execution, synthetic benchmark data, or weight erasure during export are factually inaccurate.", "type": "single", "options": ["Benchmarks are evaluated on GPUs, whereas all production models run on CPUs.", "Benchmark datasets contain only synthetic, computer-generated data that lacks realistic labels.", "Neural networks automatically lose their learned weights when exported to production formats.", "Benchmarks evaluate models on static, clean distributions without operational constraints (e.g., sub-100 ms latency budgets, memory limits, noise, and ongoing distribution shift), whereas production systems face uncurated edge cases, shifting user behavior, and hardware precision limits."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the fallacies of relying exclusively on benchmark accuracy to assess production readiness."]}, {"q": "In production ML systems engineering, selecting a model that provides a 1% higher benchmark accuracy is always preferable, even if it requires doubling inference latency and memory footprint beyond the client application's SLA.", "explanation": "False. Production model selection is a constrained multi-objective optimization problem where task accuracy must be balanced against latency, memory, power, and cost budgets; a model that violates a real-time SLA has effectively zero utility regardless of its benchmark accuracy.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate model selection trade-offs between incremental accuracy gains and production execution budgets."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate end-to-end pipeline speedup under Amdahl's Law when optimizing individual ML system components.", "You can now analyze the fallacies of relying exclusively on benchmark accuracy to assess production readiness.", "You can now explain why traditional CI/CD pipelines cannot detect silent degradation in deployed ML systems.", "You can now evaluate model selection trade-offs between incremental accuracy gains and production execution budgets."]}], "id": "v1-01-11-fallacies-and-pitfalls", "chapter": "v1-01", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-01-99-test": {"title": "Test: Introduction", "kind": "test", "summary": "Check what you learned across the whole chapter.", "blocks": [{"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-01-test", "pass_score": 0.7, "questions": [{"q": "The Viola-Jones face detection algorithm achieved real-time execution on early-2000s CPUs by using a cascade of simple rectangular features that evaluated an average of about ten features per candidate region, although the full cascade contained over 6,000 features.", "explanation": "True. Viola-Jones exemplified the statistical learning era: expert feature engineering (integral image rectangular features) and a cascaded classifier allowed early rejection of non-face regions, achieving real-time performance within narrow domains while remaining constrained by manual feature engineering when applied to new tasks.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": true, "hints": ["Think about: explain how cascaded classifiers and hand-engineered features enabled real-time inference during the statistical learning era."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **D·A·M diagnosis**?", "options": ["Across Data, Algorithm, and Machine, execution time factors as samples per task times FLOPs per sample times seconds per FLOP (where the first two multiply to total operations O), and unpacks on hardware into bytes moved over bandwidth plus operations over peak rate times utilization plus fixed overhead, with ideal overlap giving the larger of the first two plus overhead, so a faster component helps only through the share of time it changes.", "General methods that turn computation into capability have repeatedly overtaken encoded human knowledge, so each AI era traded one limiting constraint for the next and today's limit is the infrastructure: memory, bandwidth, power, and orchestration.", "Every ML system must show both that its learned behavior is trustworthy and that the machine delivers it within time, memory, energy, and cost budgets, so an accurate but slow model and a fast model trained on unrepresentative data are both failures.", "Attributing a symptom to Data, Algorithm, or Machine chooses the evidence and the class of fix, and relieving the limiting constraint moves the bottleneck across the triad, so diagnosis is a loop of diagnose, intervene, and re-measure."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**D·A·M diagnosis:** Attributing a symptom to Data, Algorithm, or Machine chooses the evidence and the class of fix, and relieving the limiting constraint moves the bottleneck across the triad, so diagnosis is a loop of diagnose, intervene, and re-measure.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is D·A·M diagnosis itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"q": "The development paradigm where engineering teams hold model architecture code relatively fixed and systematically improve dataset quality, labels, and coverage to program model behavior is known as ____ AI.", "explanation": "data-centric. It emphasizes improving the data while holding the code comparatively fixed to shape model behavior.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["data centric", "data-centric"], "hints": ["Think about: identify the term for data-centric AI versus model-centric AI."]}, {"q": "In Andrej Karpathy's Software 1.0 vs. Software 2.0 framing, how do the roles of source code, the compiler, and debugging map to machine learning workflows?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. In Software 2.0, learned weights shaped by training data act as source code, and the training loop (stochastic gradient descent) plays the role of the compiler that produces those weights from the curated examples. Debugging still traces execution, but it also expands to the data and the learned behavior. Treating Python scripts or raw datasets as source code misplaces where the program logic lives, and treating hardware or serving runtimes as compilers confuses the execution platform with the training process that produces the weights.", "type": "single", "options": ["Learned weights shaped by training data act as source code, the training loop (stochastic gradient descent) acts as the compiler, and debugging expands from execution traces to the data and learned behavior.", "Python scripts act as source code, the deep learning framework acts as the compiler, and debugging focuses on stepping through tensor operations in an interactive debugger.", "Training datasets act as source code, GPU hardware acts as the compiler, and debugging focuses on profiling memory bandwidth utilization.", "Pretrained model weights act as source code, inference serving runtimes act as the compiler, and debugging focuses on network packet inspection."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: compare the structural components of Software 1.0 with their Software 2.0 counterparts."]}, {"q": "In the D·A·M intersection landscape, the intersection between Algorithm and Machine ($\\text{A} \\cap \\text{M}$) addresses the core question of 'How to ____', focusing on how the model's mathematical work is mapped onto hardware execution and data movement efficiently.", "explanation": "Execute Efficiently. In the D·A·M taxonomy, the Algorithm-Machine intersection (A ∩ M) asks how the model's computation is carried out on physical hardware so arithmetic and movement are organized efficiently.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["Execute Efficiently"], "hints": ["Think about: identify the core engineering focus of the Algorithm-Machine intersection in the D·A·M taxonomy."]}, {"q": "An ML engineering team trains a 70-billion-parameter language model. When profiling the distributed cluster, they notice that accelerator compute engines remain idle for 45% of execution time waiting for batch tensors to be loaded from remote object storage over the network. Along which D·A·M axis does the primary limiting constraint lie, and which intersection represents the appropriate optimization space?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The bottleneck is data movement and storage throughput starving the compute engines, placing the constraint along the Data axis. The corresponding design space is the $\\text{Data} \\cap \\text{Machine}$ intersection ('How to Move Information'), which includes I/O bandwidth optimization, asynchronous prefetching, efficient storage formats, and memory hierarchy management. Optimizing mixed precision quantization ($\\text{A} \\cap \\text{M}$) accelerates compute execution but does not resolve storage starvation. Curriculum learning ($\\text{D} \\cap \\text{A}$) selects which samples to present but does not fix I/O pipeline bandwidth.", "type": "single", "options": ["Machine axis; $\\text{Algorithm} \\cap \\text{Machine}$ (mixed precision quantization and kernel fusion)", "Algorithm axis; $\\text{Data} \\cap \\text{Algorithm}$ (curriculum learning and active data selection)", "Data axis; $\\text{Data} \\cap \\text{Machine}$ (I/O pipelining, prefetching, and storage memory hierarchy)", "Workload axis; $\\text{Data} \\cap \\text{Algorithm} \\cap \\text{Machine}$ (reinforcement learning from human feedback)"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the limiting constraint in an ML system using the D·A·M taxonomy and identify the corresponding optimization intersection."]}, {"q": "Which historical transition correctly pairs an AI era with the primary systems bottleneck that limited its scalability and forced the transition to the subsequent paradigm?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Expert systems hit the knowledge acquisition bottleneck because extracting and maintaining consistent rules was bound by the serial bandwidth of human experts; statistical learning overcame this by estimating probabilities from data, but hit the feature engineering bottleneck because humans still had to manually design feature extractors (e.g., SIFT, HOG); deep learning overcame this by learning representations end-to-end from raw data. Compute throughput and GPU memory bandwidth constrained deep learning, not early symbolic or expert systems. Formal logic ambiguity was the logic bottleneck of symbolic AI, not statistical learning.", "type": "single", "options": ["Symbolic AI was limited by compute throughput, forcing the transition to expert systems; Deep Learning was limited by human rule maintenance, forcing the transition to statistical learning.", "Expert Systems were limited by GPU memory bandwidth, forcing the transition to statistical learning; Statistical Learning was limited by formal logic ambiguity, forcing the transition to deep learning.", "Statistical Learning was limited by a complete lack of training labels, forcing the transition to symbolic logic; Symbolic AI was limited by hardware integer arithmetic, forcing the transition to neural networks.", "Expert Systems were limited by the knowledge acquisition bottleneck (serial human expert elicitation bandwidth), forcing the transition to statistical learning; Statistical Learning was limited by the feature engineering bottleneck (manual extraction of hand-crafted representations), forcing the transition to deep learning."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: compare the four historical AI eras across their primary limiting systems bottlenecks."]}, {"q": "In a production ML system, if all code executes without runtime exceptions, CPU and GPU utilization are normal, and latency requirements are met, the system is guaranteed to be operating correctly.", "explanation": "False. Healthy execution telemetry shows that the software and hardware path is functioning, but it does not guarantee good predictions. In an ML system, the code can run correctly and on time while the live data has drifted away from the training distribution, causing silent degradation in prediction quality.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: explain why traditional software health metrics such as uptime and exception logging are insufficient to verify ML system correctness."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Data defines behavior**?", "options": ["Training data plus the optimization procedure produce the program, so changing the dataset changes system behavior as surely as changing source code, and debugging, versioning, and review must extend to the data.", "General methods that turn computation into capability have repeatedly overtaken encoded human knowledge, so each AI era traded one limiting constraint for the next and today's limit is the infrastructure: memory, bandwidth, power, and orchestration.", "Attributing a symptom to Data, Algorithm, or Machine chooses the evidence and the class of fix, and relieving the limiting constraint moves the bottleneck across the triad, so diagnosis is a loop of diagnose, intervene, and re-measure.", "ML adds a failure route in which code, runtime, and health checks stay green while predictive quality falls as the operating population shifts or training and serving transforms diverge, and locally accuracy falls by a fitted sensitivity times the divergence (eq-degradation), where divergence prompts investigation and labeled outcomes establish loss."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "**Data defines behavior:** Training data plus the optimization procedure produce the program, so changing the dataset changes system behavior as surely as changing source code, and debugging, versioning, and review must extend to the data.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Data defines behavior itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"q": "A computer vision test suite evaluates a \\$224×224 RGB image classifier on 50,000 validation images. Why does passing 100% of these test cases still leave a substantial 'verification gap' in production?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The verification gap, the disparity between what a test set covers and the inputs a deployed system meets, arises because the input space of \\$224{\\times}224$ 8-bit RGB images contains \\$256^{150{,}528}$ possible configurations (a number with over 300,000 digits in base 10), whereas a 50,000-image test set evaluates a vanishingly small fraction. Predeployment testing provides statistical evidence over sampled inputs, not exhaustive mathematical proof. Explanations invoking integer precision describe quantization effects rather than the fundamental input-space disparity. Explanations suggesting batch-size limits or backward pass requirements in production confuse inference serving with training mechanics.", "type": "single", "options": ["Validation sets evaluate floating-point weights, whereas production inference engines always run in integer precision.", "The total input space of possible pixel configurations (\\$256^150,528, spanning over 300,000 decimal digits) vastly exceeds the sample coverage of any finite test set, making exhaustive testing mathematically impossible.", "Convolutional neural networks cannot generalize beyond the exact batch size used during validation testing.", "Test suites only evaluate forward inference passes, whereas production systems must continuously execute backward gradient updates."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: calculate and explain the mathematical origin of the verification gap in high-dimensional ML systems."]}, {"q": "Place the four historical AI engineering eras in chronological order based on when their primary paradigm dominated, and identify the key bottleneck that constrained each era:", "explanation": "The correct order is (3) -> (2) -> (4) -> (1).\n- (3) Symbolic AI Era (1950s–1970s): Constrained by the logic bottleneck (brittle hand-coded rules unable to handle real-world ambiguity).\n- (2) Expert Systems Era (1970s–1980s): Constrained by the knowledge acquisition bottleneck (serial human expert elicitation bandwidth).\n- (4) Statistical Learning Era (1990s–2000s): Constrained by the feature engineering bottleneck (manual extraction of hand-crafted features prior to statistical classification).\n- (1) Deep Learning Era (2010s–present): Constrained by the compute and infrastructure bottleneck (hardware scaling, memory bandwidth, and distributed coordination).", "type": "order", "items": ["Symbolic AI Era", "Expert Systems Era", "Statistical Learning Era", "Deep Learning Era"], "hints": ["Think about: classify the chronological progression of AI engineering eras and their respective systems bottlenecks."]}, {"q": "Place the six ML project responsibilities in their initial progression from defining the task to maintaining the deployed system:", "explanation": "The correct order is (4) -> (5) -> (2) -> (1) -> (6) -> (3).\n- (4) Problem Definition: Specify the task, operating population, constraints, and acceptance requirements.\n- (5) Data Collection & Preparation: Gather representative evidence and construct the inputs used for learning.\n- (2) Model Development & Training: Choose and train a model within the resource budget.\n- (1) Evaluation & Validation: Assess task quality and system requirements under a stated protocol.\n- (6) Deployment & Integration: Integrate the validated artifact into its operating environment.\n- (3) Monitoring & Maintenance: Gather production evidence and diagnose whether earlier work needs revision.\nThis is an initial progression, not a rule that every alert repeats every stage.", "type": "order", "items": ["Problem Definition", "Data Collection & Preparation", "Model Development & Training", "Evaluation & Validation", "Deployment & Integration", "Monitoring & Maintenance"], "hints": ["Think about: classify the sequential stages of the ML system lifecycle."]}], "mode": "test"}], "id": "v1-01-99-test", "chapter": "v1-01", "minutes": 10}});
